Greetings. It is a pleasure to assist you. 

**QuickSort** is an extraordinarily efficient, comparison-based, divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm. Developed by British computer scientist Tony Hoare in 1959, it remains one of the most widely utilized sorting algorithms in modern computing.

---

### The Underlying Mechanics

QuickSort operates on a simple yet profound premise:

1. **Pivot Selection:** Choose an element from the array to act as the *pivot*.
2. **Partitioning:** Rearrange the array such that:
   - All elements less than the pivot reside on its left.
   - All elements greater than the pivot reside on its right.
   - The pivot occupies its final, sorted position.
3. **Recursive Step:** Recursively apply the preceding steps to the sub-arrays to the left and right of the pivot until the entire collection is sorted.

---

### Implementation in TypeScript

Below is an elegant, in-place implementation utilizing TypeScript generics and the classic **Lomuto partition scheme**.

```typescript
/**
 * Swaps two elements in an array in-place.
 */
function swap<T>(arr: T[], i: number, j: number): void {
  const temp = arr[i];
  arr[i] = arr[j];
  arr[j] = temp;
}

/**
 * Partitions the array around a pivot element.
 * Elements <= pivot are moved to the left; elements > pivot to the right.
 *
 * @param arr - The array to be partitioned.
 * @param low - Starting index of the partition range.
 * @param high - Ending index of the partition range.
 * @returns The final index of the pivot.
 */
function partition<T>(
  arr: T[],
  low: number,
  high: number,
  compare: (a: T, b: T) => number
): number {
  // Choose the rightmost element as the pivot
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;

  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    // If the current element is smaller than or equal to the pivot
    if (compare(arr[j], pivot) <= 0) {
      i++;
      swap(arr, i, j);
    }
  }

  // Place the pivot in its correct sorted position
  swap(arr, i + 1, high);
  return i + 1;
}

/**
 * The internal recursive QuickSort function.
 */
function quickSortInternal<T>(
  arr: T[],
  low: number,
  high: number,
  compare: (a: T, b: T) => number
): void {
  if (low < high) {
    // pi is the partitioning index; arr[pi] is now in the correct place
    const pi = partition(arr, low, high, compare);

    // Recursively sort elements before and after partition
    quickSortInternal(arr, low, pi - 1, compare);
    quickSortInternal(arr, pi + 1, high, compare);
  }
}

/**
 * Public QuickSort API. Sorts an array in-place.
 *
 * @param array - The array to sort.
 * @param compare - An optional comparator function (defaults to standard '<' / '>' comparison).
 * @returns The sorted array reference.
 */
export function quickSort<T>(
  array: T[],
  compare: (a: T, b: T) => number = (a, b) => (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0)
): T[] {
  if (array.length <= 1) return array;
  quickSortInternal(array, 0, array.length - 1, compare);
  return array;
}
```

---

### Demonstration & Usage

```typescript
// Example 1: Sorting numbers
const numbers = [64, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90, 1];
console.log("Original:", numbers);
quickSort(numbers);
console.log("Sorted:", numbers);
// Output: [1, 11, 12, 22, 25, 64, 90]

// Example 2: Sorting custom objects with a comparator
interface User {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

const users: User[] = [
  { name: "Ada", age: 36 },
  { name: "Alan", age: 41 },
  { name: "Grace", age: 85 },
  { name: "Claude", age: 28 },
];

quickSort(users, (a, b) => a.age - b.age);
console.log("Sorted by age:", users);
```

---

### Algorithmic Complexity

| Metric | Complexity | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Best Case Time** | $\mathcal{O}(n \log n)$ | Occurs when the pivot consistently partitions the array into balanced halves. |
| **Average Case Time** | $\mathcal{O}(n \log n)$ | Typical performance across random permutations. |
| **Worst Case Time** | $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ | Occurs when the pivot is consistently the minimum or maximum element (e.g., already sorted array with naive pivot selection). |
| **Space Complexity** | $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ | Auxiliary call stack space for recursion (in-place). |

*Optimization Note:* To mitigate the $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ worst-case scenario in production environments, it is recommended to implement a **median-of-three** pivot selection or randomized pivot selection.

